    layer "doctrine"    state="evidenced" evidence="skills/verify-the-published-tree/SKILL.md, registered in the router" reaches="the skill teaches that a published document is never edited, only superseded"
    layer "enforcement" state="evidenced" evidence="98 tests pass, four of them against a real repository; drift and unreadability both fail closed" reaches="verify-against-the-claim and verify-on-push"
    layer "harness"     state="evidenced" evidence=".github/workflows/delivery-graph.yml — check, test, verify and ready on every push, fetch-depth 0" reaches="the CI path, so verification runs without anyone invoking it"
    layer "docs"        state="skipped" \
        because="the slice itself declares this layer reaches nothing — it READS the published tree \
            and adds nothing to it. Skipped with the slice's own words as the rationale, which is \
            what skipped-phase-without-rationale asks for one level up"
    layer-set sealed=#true at="2026-08-21T15:15:00Z"

    phase "design-ux" state="complete" required-by="enforcement" \
        produced="what a failing check SAYS. Three different drifts — edited, removed, added — each \
            with its own sentence, because `this file changed` does not tell a reader whether they \
            are looking at a hand edit, a deletion, or a file that was never published at all. And \
            `cannot be verified` reads differently from `failed`, since one is about the document \
            and the other is about the clone"
    phase "design-system" state="complete" required-by="enforcement" \
        produced="Unverifiable as a THIRD outcome rather than an error case. A boolean `verified` \
            would have made skipping the natural implementation of `could not check`, and skipping \
            quietly is how verification gets switched off without a decision. The type makes the \
            wrong answer unspellable"
    phase "implement" state="complete" required-by="enforcement · harness" \
        produced="praxis-core::verify, `praxis verify-published`, the CI workflow, nine unit tests \
            and four end-to-end tests against a real repository"
    phase "learn" state="complete" required-by="always" produced="four findings, one of them a contradiction between the config and a slice" \
        note="never skippable — it is the phase that judges the others"
    phase "teach" state="complete" required-by="doctrine" produced="skills/verify-the-published-tree/SKILL.md" \
        taught="the-next-agent" \
        taught="the-next-agent"

    finding "Z1" state="open" size="answered" \
        text="P1 is answered. It has said since the graph was seeded that no slice reaches all four \
            layers and that `harness` is reached by nothing. TS.260820.11 declares all four and this \
            iteration evidenced three of them, skipping `docs` on the slice's own stated grounds" \
        note="one slice in nineteen reaches harness, and it reached it by needing CI rather than by \
            anyone deciding the layer should be exercised. That is the honest reading: the layer is \
            not neglected, it is rarely relevant, and a frame about a METHOD touches it once"

    finding "Z2" state="open" size="observation" \
        text="C2 named two tests and one of them could not be written. `changing an admission \
            condition` cannot reach `verify` — the function takes what git holds and what the tree \
            holds, and there is no parameter for a rule" \
        note="a stronger settlement than the claim asked for, recorded rather than quietly \
            substituted. The claim wanted evidence that a rule change does not break the check; what \
            exists is a shape in which a rule change cannot be an input. The same pattern as X3 — a \
            claim settled by impossibility rather than demonstration, which is better and less \
            guarded"

    finding "Z3" state="open" size="owed-work" \
        text="config.kdl defines the `harness` layer as owning the six install-path manifests. A CI \
            workflow is none of them, and TS.260820.11 says harness reaches `the CI path`. The \
            config and the slice disagree about what the layer IS" \
        obliges="praxis/config.kdl — either harness owns the CI path too, or the slice names a \
            different layer" \
        note="taken as the slice states it, because what makes something harness is that it runs \
            WITHOUT invocation rather than which directory it sits in. But that is a reading, and \
            nothing checks a layer's evidence against what the config says the layer owns"

    finding "Z4" state="open" size="observation" \
        text="this is the first iteration to run tests against a REAL repository — temp directory, \
            git init, a real commit, the actual binary. Every prior test built its record as a \
            string and called a pure function" \
        note="the pure tests are faster, clearer and cannot answer the only question that mattered \
            here: whether `git ls-tree` and `git show` against a recorded commit do what the frame \
            assumes. Four tests took what the pure ones could not reach, and they are the slowest in \
            the suite by a factor of twenty"
